<WikiLayout title="WARDOGS Wiki Contributors" description="Editorial ownership, contributor roles, review responsibilities, and accountability standards for WARDOGS Wiki." canonicalPath="/contributors/" pageType="AboutPage">
  <h1>WARDOGS Wiki Contributors</h1>
  <p>WARDOGS Wiki is published by the <strong>WARDOGS Wiki Editorial Team</strong>. The project uses role-based attribution instead of inventing personal credentials. Every published reference remains accountable to the same sourcing, versioning, and correction rules.</p>

  <h2>Editorial roles</h2>
  <h3>Research and verification</h3>
  <p>Researchers locate first-party material, identify version boundaries, and flag facts that cannot be confirmed. They do not convert community assumptions into official claims.</p>
  <h3>Database maintenance</h3>
  <p>Database editors keep names, classifications, relationships, and patch context consistent across entity records. Unknown values remain explicitly unconfirmed.</p>
  <h3>Guide review</h3>
  <p>Guide reviewers test whether advice follows from the documented system, whether alternatives are represented fairly, and whether tactical interpretation is clearly separated from factual rules.</p>
  <h3>Technical publishing</h3>
  <p>Technical maintainers protect stable routes, canonical URLs, structured data, accessibility, responsive layouts, and internal-link integrity during releases.</p>

  <h2>Review ownership</h2>
  <p>Articles identify the editorial team as the reviewer and display the most recent review date available in the content record. This is an accountability signal, not a claim that every mechanic was re-tested on that date.</p>

  <h2>How standards are applied</h2>
  <p>Contributors follow the <a href="/methodology/">research methodology</a>, <a href="/sources/">source standards</a>, and <a href="/editorial-policy/">editorial policy</a>. Commercial separation is documented in the <a href="/advertising-policy/">advertising policy</a>.</p>
</WikiLayout>
